From f1f5c5d43030c7795662621c22d4f9b048d32cd7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Silas=20M=C3=BCller?= <github@silasmueller.de>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 08:52:25 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] nvidia: add a display reserve for kernel display allocations
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A display allocation of a kernel client (NVKMS) has no fallback: a scanout
surface must be physically contiguous video memory, and nvidia-drm falls
back to system memory only for NO_SCANOUT buffers.  A process that sizes
its pools to the free video memory, such as a game sizing them to the
Vulkan memory budget, can leave no room for the next surface.  The refusal
leaves Xwayland or the compositor without a buffer and the display stops
updating.

Add an optional reserve, enabled with the RmDisplayReserve registry key and
off by default.  The reserve is a set of chunks allocated on the memory
manager's internal client with the attributes NVKMS uses for a scanout
surface (NVOS32_TYPE_PRIMARY, physically contiguous, ISO), so it counts as
used video memory and each freed chunk leaves room of the shape a scanout
surface needs.

The reserve is sized from the display surfaces that exist: RM tracks the
video memory allocated with ISO bandwidth guarantees and without
NO_SCANOUT, by any client, and the largest of these is one scanout surface
of the largest mode in use.  The reserve holds five such surfaces and four
256x256 cursors (RmDisplayReserveSizeMB sets another size), cut into chunks
of one surface, and nothing while no display surface exists.  Each refill
cuts it for the largest surface at that time; a surface larger than the
current cut starts a refill at once, so the reserve follows mode changes
and hotplug.

When a scanout allocation of a kernel client (kernel privilege, ISO,
physically contiguous, not NO_SCANOUT) fails in vidmemConstruct with
NV_ERR_NO_MEMORY, free chunks that cover it and retry the allocation in the
same call.  Allocations hold the API lock for writing unless RmRoApiLock
enables read-only allocations, so no other allocation can take the freed
memory between the free and the retry.  The reserve refills when a surface
it made room for is freed, and otherwise on a backoff of 1 to 30 seconds.
It is emptied in memmgrStatePreUnload, before suspend and unload, and
refilled from memmgrStatePostLoad, after load and resume.

Signed-off-by: Silas Müller <github@silasmueller.de>
